\section{Give a Spelling an Identity, Not a Meaning}

Consider two occurrences of \texttt{count} in an outer function and a
third occurrence in a nested function that declares its own
\texttt{count}. The lexer can assign all three the same symbol.
The resolver must not assign all three the same binding. One identity
answers ``what name did the user write?''; the other answers ``which
declaration does this use select?'' Appel's Tiger implementation makes
string equality cheap by mapping each spelling to a symbol and then
uses symbols as keys in separate environments \cite{appel2004modern}.
The indirection is useful precisely because the same alphabet of names
can serve different scopes and namespaces. \Cref{ch:ext-420}
uses those IDs as keys for lookup, shadowing, and restoration.

Use an opaque \texttt{SymbolId}, minted by a pool, rather than exposing
an integer conversion as a public constructor. A token contains this
ID as well as a source span. The span lets the diagnostic printer show
the exact input even if the language admits multiple spellings for a
name. For example, if canonical Unicode normalization is part of the
language, apply it before interning and preserve the source bytes in
the source snapshot. If the language instead compares identifier bytes
literally, normalization changes the meaning of a program and must not
be applied. Homograph warnings, if supported, are a separate analysis;
similar-looking names need not compare equal.

\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{lll}
\hline
Identity & Example & Validity domain \\
\hline
Spelling ID & \texttt{count} in the name pool & Owning pool lifetime \\
Binding ID & Inner declaration of \texttt{count} & Resolver's declaration store \\
Source span & Bytes of one use of \texttt{count} & One file revision \\
Artifact ID & Name at index 6 in an object file & Artifact's own name table \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

\section{Keep the Reverse Lookup Independent of Buckets}

A useful layout has three pieces: a byte store containing canonical
spellings, a dense vector of byte descriptors, and a hash index from
candidate hash to symbol IDs. A descriptor records offset and length,
so the byte store can move on growth without changing the ID. Reverse
lookup indexes the vector directly. Forward lookup computes a hash and
then tests the bytes of candidates. Neither the hash nor the index
bucket is a symbol ID. Two different spellings can have the same hash,
and rebuilding the index at a new capacity can move every bucket.

In \Cref{fig:visual-419}, two spellings deliberately collide in bucket
\(h\). Growing the pool rebuilds the arrows on the left but leaves the
arrows on the right alone. A conventional open-addressed table can
encode these candidates as a probe sequence instead of a chain; it
still needs a full equality test before returning an existing ID.

