% Annotated walkthrough
\section{Make Insertion the Only Way to Mint an ID}

\begin{verbatim}
intern(pool, source_bytes):
    key := canonical_name(source_bytes)
    h := hash(key, pool.seed)
    with pool.write_lock:
        for id in pool.index.candidates(h):
            if bytes_at(pool, id) == key:
                return id

        id := checked_next_id(pool.descriptors.length)
        reserve_descriptor_and_index_capacity(pool, h)
        reserve_bytes(pool, key.length)
        offset := pool.bytes.length
        pool.bytes.append(key)
        pool.descriptors.append((offset, key.length))
        pool.index.add(h, id)
        return id

spelling(pool, id):
    require id belongs to pool
    with pool.read_lock:
        return copy(bytes_at(pool, id))
\end{verbatim}

The candidate search must occur under the write lock, or be repeated
there if an optimistic lookup missed. If two worker threads both miss
and append without rechecking, equality on IDs is no longer a valid
test for equality on names. The reserve steps arrange for the published
update to require no further allocation in this sketch. An implementation
with fallible operations later in the sequence needs to roll back
partial insertion or build the entry privately before publishing it.
The reverse lookup copies while the lock protects the backing array;
a borrowed slice into a relocatable byte store cannot outlive the lock.
Immutable separately allocated chunks are an alternative if slice
borrowing is important.

Hashing an input of length \(k\) still costs \(O(k)\). The gain comes
after interning, when resolution, occurrence searches, and IR attribute
lookups compare or hash small IDs. With suitably distributed hashes and
a controlled load factor, the candidate count is small on average; a
forced-collision test is still worth keeping. To assess memory, count
distinct canonical bytes, descriptors, and index capacity separately.
An input with a fresh name at every occurrence may pay more for
interning than it would for spans alone. Avoid interning literal
bodies just because identifier names already use a pool.

\section{Define What Stable Means at the Export Boundary}

A pool with append-only descriptors guarantees stability across
capacity changes within one lifetime. It does not guarantee that
the name \texttt{count} gets the same integer after a clean build.
Discovery order changes under parallel parsing, and restoring a saved
artifact might start with a different subset of the names. For a
deterministic export, collect the names that the artifact actually
uses, sort their canonical bytes with a specified comparison, give
them artifact-local indices, and rewrite every emitted reference
through the translation map. On import, intern the serialized bytes in
the receiving pool before interpreting any reference. Sorting only
the printed name table while leaving raw pool IDs in bytecode is
not a valid translation.

Separate pools also require a bridge. If worker \(A\)'s number 12
means \texttt{count} while worker \(B\)'s number 12 means \texttt{size},
their elements must not be compared by integer alone. Use a branded ID
type when the language supports it, or store/check a pool tag at
boundaries. When merging, construct a worker-to-owner ID map once and
apply it to the whole transferred representation, including any side
tables of symbol-keyed metadata. Rehashing the worker's local table
does nothing to make its IDs valid elsewhere.

\section{Plan for Retention Before Adding Eviction}

A one-shot compiler can usually free the whole pool after emitting
the artifact. An editor service that interns across thousands of edits
can accumulate long-abandoned misspellings. Start with an explicit
pool lifetime such as a workspace session or document epoch. Before
introducing eviction, identify caches, snapshots, and diagnostics
that can retain the ID. If an evicted index is immediately reused,
an old token could silently name a different spelling. Epoch changes
with explicit remapping, or generational handles with checked lookup,
trade that silent error for a visible invalidation boundary. If the
contract instead claims identity across all simultaneously live
snapshots, reclamation must preserve that contract.

Test the contract by forcing a resize, forcing unequal keys to hash
identically, and parsing the same inputs in different worker orders.
Check that in-memory equality agrees with canonical byte equality
inside each pool and that final artifacts agree byte for byte across
worker orders. Add a separate fixture with two same-spelled
declarations. Their spelling IDs must match and their binding IDs
must differ even after both sets of IDs have been exported and read
back. These assertions detect different failures and should not use
one another as their test oracle.

\section{Budget the Width of the Descriptor Fields}

Suppose a workspace keeps an average of twelve canonical bytes
for each of five million distinct names. The raw spellings then
take about sixty million bytes, not counting allocation slack.
An eight-byte descriptor takes forty million bytes more, before
any space for probing or chaining. This estimate suggests a useful
layout change: keep the descriptor unpadded if possible and avoid a
separate heap allocation for each tiny spelling. It does not
justify truncating the byte offset to fit into a thirty-two-bit
field. A workspace with fewer but longer names can exhaust the byte
offset before it exhausts the ID. Check both limits independently,
including any reserved sentinel used for missing names.

A sharded pool can reduce contention, but the shard assignment
must be a function of the canonical name: all equal keys must
consult the same shard before choosing an ID. Encoding shard and
local index together preserves opaque equality, at the cost of
dividing the available bits and complicating vectors indexed by
symbol. Measure the time spent hashing and copying separately from
time blocked on the lock before adding sharding. If most work
occurs outside this lock, a more elaborate ID layout can impose a
lasting cost on downstream tables for very little throughput gain.
